entities as status indians when  they came home they were enfranchised  and  no longer were recognized as status  indians they weren't provided the same  treatment as other veterans so creating  a day to acknowledge that is very  important  there is a long history of racism in the  canadian armed forces during the first  world war hundreds of black canadians  eager to enlist were turned away told it  was a white man's war they formed the  first and only all-black battalion in  the canadian military ross lord explains  what the segregated unit did and why a  long overdue apology is being planned  you know according to historical records  on that day it was a damp  rainy cold day and a cold shoulder for  the 600 black soldiers who sailed from  halifax harbor in 1917 sharing the  voyage to england with white troops and  here they're going into something they  don't really know what they're facing  with people who don't want them  the men felt the sting of racism every  day but especially after trying to  enlist in the war effort when the black  young men arrived they were told this is  not your war it's a white man's war  they were turned away eventually they  formed the number two construction  battalion known as the black battalion  the only segregated military unit in  canadian history as it turned out they  did play a crucial part in the war  building rail lines trenches and bridges  for the soldiers they wanted to fight  alongside when you look at the  remarkable aspect of those men wanting  to serve when they really weren't  wanted you know  as we commonly say here you know they  had to fight for the right to fight  that took a lot of inner strength for  decades their unique story was forgotten  but advocates and descendants mounted  their own determined effort first  dignifying derelict grave markers then  for a wider acknowledgement of their  ancestors and the racism they  encountered  the federal government is now planning a  public apology for next july that 